-- title:
--   Pinned Galois transport on relative Pic⁰ equals τ(s)
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$ and $M$ with $5 \le M$ (`hM`) and $p \nmid M$ (`hpM`). Let $L$ be a field of characteristic zero which is a cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$ of type $\{p\}$, let $\zeta \in L$ be a primitive $p$-th root of unity, and let $K$ be an intermediate field of $L \subseteq L((q))$ with $K =$ [`ModularCurve.laurentBaseChange L (ModularCurve.x1FunctionField (M * p))`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L103) (`hK`), i.e. the $L$-subfield of the Laurent series field generated by the coefficientwise image of the function field of $X_1(Mp)$ over $\mathbb{Q}$. Let $A$ be a discrete valuation domain with $L$ as fraction field, with $p$ in its maximal ideal (`hAp`) and $\zeta$ in the image of $A$ (`hζA`), and let $A$ act on $K$ compatibly with $L$. Let $j \in K$ be nonzero whose Laurent expansion is [`ModularCurve.coeffEmb L ModularCurve.jq`](def/ModularCurve_LaurentCoeff.html#L81) (`hj`). The curve in play is the two-chart model [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.modelTo A K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L252) of $X_1(Mp)$ over $\operatorname{Spec} A$, assumed proper.
--
--   Special-fibre data. Let $k$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic $p$ with an $A$-algebra structure, and let $c_1 : C_1 \to \operatorname{Spec} k$, $c_2 : C_2 \to \operatorname{Spec} k$ be proper, smooth of relative dimension $1$ and geometrically integral. Let $i_1, i_2$ be closed immersions of $C_1$, $C_2$ into the base change of the model to $k$, each over $k$; `hcover` requires every point of that base change to lie in the image of $i_1$ or of $i_2$; `hred` requires the scheme-theoretic intersection `pullback i₁.1 i₂.1` to be reduced, and `hn`, `hn0` give it cardinality $n > 0$.
--
--   Sections. $\varepsilon$ is a section of the model over $A$, $\varepsilon_1$, $\varepsilon_2$ are sections of $c_1$, $c_2$ over $k$, and `hε₁` requires $\varepsilon_1$ followed by $i_1$ to be the base change `sectionBaseChange k ε` of $\varepsilon$.
--
--   Relative $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ data. $D$ is a `RelativePic0Designation` for the model: a scheme $D.P$ with a structure morphism $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ to $\operatorname{Spec} A$ and a zero section. `hrep` asserts that $D$ represents the subfunctor of rigidified line bundles on the model cut out by `algEquivZeroCut` (the pointwise condition `FibrewiseAlgEquivZero`), that is: a Poincaré rigidified bundle on $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ satisfying the condition, a unique classifying morphism for every such bundle on every $A$-scheme, and triviality of the pull-back along the zero section. `hsm` and `hsep` require $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ to be smooth and separated. `hreps` is the corresponding representability witness for the base change of the model to $k$, the section `sectionBaseChange k ε` and `D.baseChange k`, and `hPk` pins its Poincaré bundle to the base change to $k$ of the pull-back of the relative Poincaré bundle along `pullback.fst D.toBase (specMap A k)`. Likewise $D_1$, $D_2$ with witnesses `hrep₁`, `hrep₂` represent the analogous functors for $(c_1, \varepsilon_1)$ and $(c_2, \varepsilon_2)$. The morphism $\nu_2$ from $(D.\mathrm{baseChange}\,k).\mathrm{toBase}$ to $D_2.\mathrm{toBase}$ over $k$ is pinned by `hν₂`: for every $k$-scheme $T$ and every $T$-point $a$ of $(D.\mathrm{baseChange}\,k).\mathrm{toBase}$, the pull-back of the Poincaré bundle of $D_2$ along $a$ followed by $\nu_2$ is isomorphic to the rigidification, along `rigSection c₂ t ε₂` and `pullback.snd c₂ t`, of the pull-back along `curveChange i₂.1 i₂.2 t` of the pull-back of the Poincaré bundle of `hreps` along $a$.
--
--   Generic-fibre data. $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra structures embed $A$ and $L$ into $\overline{\mathbb{Q}} =$ `AlgebraicClosure ℚ` compatibly. The hypotheses `hsmL`, `hgiL` require the base change of the model to $L$ to be smooth of relative dimension $1$ and geometrically integral, and `hprL`, `hgcL` require `pullback.snd D.toBase (specMap A L)` to be proper and geometrically connected. $M_\eta$ is a `CurveModel` over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ for the function field [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L182) (an integral, proper, smooth relative-dimension-one curve together with a ring isomorphism of that field with its function field and a bijection between closed points and places); $e_\eta$ is an isomorphism from $M_\eta.C$ to the base change of the model to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ commuting with the structure morphisms (`heη`), the preimage under $e_\eta$ followed by `pullback.fst` of the image of the finite chart is nonempty, and `hMηpin` requires that for each element $a$ of [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.chartAlgFin A K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L135) the function obtained by pulling $a$ back along $e_\eta$ followed by `pullback.fst` and taking its germ at the generic point of that open, read in [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionFieldBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L182) through $M_\eta.\mathrm{ffEquiv}$, has Laurent expansion the coefficientwise image along $L \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ of the expansion of $a$. The hypothesis `hgal` requires that for every $g \in \operatorname{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ fixing the image of $L$ pointwise and every two $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points $x, x'$ of $M_\eta.C$, if $x'$ followed by $e_\eta$ and `pullback.fst` equals $\operatorname{Spec}(g)$ followed by $x$ followed by the same, then the place attached to $x'$ by `Mη.pointEquivPlace` is the translate of that of $x$ under [`ModularCurve.arithmeticGalois (ModularCurve.x1FunctionField (M * p)) g`](def/ModularCurve_ArithmeticGalois.html#L54). Finally `hin` and `hcomm` are the Hecke–diamond input and commutation hypotheses [`ModularCurve.HeckeDiamondInputsAll (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L58) and [`ModularCurve.HeckeDiamondCommuteBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L54).
--
--   Galois action on the base. $\operatorname{Gal}(L/\mathbb{Q})$ acts on $A$ by ring automorphisms, and `hΓA` requires this action to be compatible with $A \to L$.
--
--   Special-fibre group dictionary. $G$ is a [`ModularCurve.JOneP.NeronSpecialFibreGeom p`](def/ModularCurve_JOnePGeom.html#L9): abelian groups $G.\mathrm{J0s}$, $G.\mathrm{JI}$, $G.\mathrm{JE}$, a subgroup `torus` of $G.\mathrm{J0s}$, and a surjective homomorphism $G.\mathrm{proj}$ to the product with kernel the torus. The bijections `pts`, `ptsI`, `ptsE` identify $G.\mathrm{J0s}$, $G.\mathrm{JI}$, $G.\mathrm{JE}$ with the $k$-points of $(D.\mathrm{baseChange}\,k).\mathrm{toBase}$, $D_1.\mathrm{toBase}$, $D_2.\mathrm{toBase}$; `hadd`, `haddI`, `haddE` require each of them to turn addition into the tensor product of the corresponding pull-backs of the respective Poincaré bundles; `hproj` requires, for every $x \in G.\mathrm{J0s}$, that `ptsI (G.proj x).1` be `pts x` followed by `RepresentsRelSubPic.pullbackHom i₁.1 i₁.2 hε₁ hreps hrep₁.some` and that `ptsE (G.proj x).2` be `pts x` followed by $\nu_2$.
--
--   Generic dictionary and operators. `gpts` is a bijection between [`ModularCurve.JOne (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L186) (the degree-zero divisor class group of the geometric curve) and the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points of $D.\mathrm{toBase}$; `φ` assigns to each element of [`ModularCurve.HeckeAlgOne`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L16) $= \mathbb{Z}[X_i : i \in \mathrm{Primes} \sqcup \mathbb{N}]$ an endomorphism of $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ over $A$; `τ` assigns to each $s \in \operatorname{Gal}(L/\mathbb{Q})$ a morphism $D.P \to D.P$ over the twisted structure morphism $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ followed by $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the ring automorphism of $A$ induced by $s$. The hypotheses require: `hφmul`, each $\varphi_t$ is additive for the relative group law supplied by `hrep.some` with respect to `algEquivZeroGroupCut`; `hφpts`, for the Hecke module structure [`ModularCurve.heckeModuleOneBar (M * p)`](def/ModularCurve_X1HeckeModule.html#L129), $\mathrm{gpts}(t \cdot x)$ is $\mathrm{gpts}(x)$ followed by $\varphi_t$; `hτ1` and `hτmul`, $\tau(1)$ is the identity of $D.P$ and $\tau(ss')$ is $\tau(s)$ followed by $\tau(s')$; `hτφ`, $\tau(s)$ and $\varphi_t$ commute; `hgadd`, `gpts` is additive for the relative group law; `hτpts`, for $\sigma' \in \operatorname{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb{Q}}/\mathbb{Q})$ and $s \in \operatorname{Gal}(L/\mathbb{Q})$ with $\sigma'$ restricting to $s$ on $L$, and every $x$, $\mathrm{gpts}(\sigma' \cdot x)$ is $\operatorname{Spec}(\sigma')$ followed by $\mathrm{gpts}(x)$ followed by $\tau(s^{-1})$.
--
--   Abel–Jacobi pinning. `hDL` is the representability witness over $L$ for `D.baseChange L`; `ajL` is a morphism from the base change of the model to $L$ to $(D.\mathrm{baseChange}\,L).\mathrm{toBase}$ over $L$; `kL` is a morphism between the base changes of the model to $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ and to $L$ with the two compatibilities `hkL₁`, `hkL₂`; `ajbar` is a morphism $M_\eta.C \to D.P$ and $\bar\varepsilon$ a $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-point of $M_\eta.C$. The hypotheses require: `hPL`, the Poincaré bundle of `hDL` is isomorphic to the base change to $L$ of the pull-back of the relative Poincaré bundle along `pullback.fst D.toBase (specMap A L)`; `hajLε`, `ajL` carries `sectionBaseChange L ε` to the zero section of `D.baseChange L`; `hajL`, for every field $K'$, every morphism $t$ from $\operatorname{Spec} K'$ to $\operatorname{Spec} L$ and every $K'$-point $x$ of the curve over $L$, the pull-back of the Poincaré bundle of `hDL` along $x$ followed by `ajL` is isomorphic to the line bundle of the relative effective Cartier divisor of the point $x$ tensored with the ideal module of that of $t$ followed by `sectionBaseChange L ε`, i.e. to $\mathcal{O}(x - \varepsilon)$; `hajbar`, `ajbar` equals $e_\eta$ followed by `kL`, `ajL` and `pullback.fst D.toBase (specMap A L)`, and `hajbar_over` its compatibility over the base; `hεbar`, $\bar\varepsilon$ followed by $e_\eta$ and `pullback.fst` is $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $A \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ followed by $\varepsilon$, and `hεbar_aj`, $\bar\varepsilon$ followed by `ajbar` is that morphism followed by the zero section of $D$; `hpts_aj`, for all $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points $x$, $s$ of $M_\eta.C$ with $s$ pinned to $\varepsilon$ as in `hεbar`, there is a degree-zero divisor $Dv$ on the geometric curve equal to $[\text{place of } x] - [\text{place of } s]$ with $\mathrm{gpts}$ of its class equal to $x$ followed by `ajbar`.
--
--   The element and the pinned transport. Fix one $s \in \operatorname{Gal}(L/\mathbb{Q})$. Let $u$ be an endomorphism of the two-chart model covering $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the automorphism of $A$ given by $s^{-1}$ (`hu`), let $\rho_u$ be a ring automorphism of [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.chartAlgFin A K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L135) acting on Laurent expansions by coefficientwise application of $s^{-1}$ (`hρu`) and compatible with $u$ on the finite chart, in the sense that [`ModularCurve.TwoChart.ιFin A K j`](def/ModularCurve_TwoChartModel.html#L231) followed by $u$ equals $\operatorname{Spec}(\rho_u)$ followed by that chart morphism (`huρ`); `hsinv` requires $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the automorphism of $s$ followed by that of $s^{-1}$ to be the identity of $\operatorname{Spec} A$. Finally $N$ is a morphism $D.P \to D.P$ over $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ followed by $\operatorname{Spec}$ of the automorphism given by $s$, pinned by `hN`: for every $A$-scheme $T$ with structure morphism $t$ and every $T$-point $a$ of $D.\mathrm{toBase}$ over $t$, the pull-back of the Poincaré bundle of `hrep.some` along $a$ followed by $N$ (a $T$-point over $t$ followed by $\operatorname{Spec}(s)$) is isomorphic to the rigidification, along `rigSection` for $\varepsilon$ over the twisted base and the second projection, of the pull-back along the base change `pullback.map … u (𝟙 T) …` of $u$ of the pull-back of that Poincaré bundle along $a$.
--
--   Conclusion: the underlying morphism of $\tau(s)$ equals that of $N$, i.e. `(τ s).1 = N.1` as morphisms $D.P \to D.P$.
--
--   This is the rigidity step identifying the semilinear Galois automorphism $\tau(s)$ of the relative $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ model of $X_1(Mp)$ over the ring of integers $A$ of $\mathbb{Q}(\zeta_p)$ with the morphism $N$ pinned as pull-back of rigidified line bundles along a model endomorphism $u$ covering $\operatorname{Spec} s^{-1}$: the two morphisms agree because they agree on $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-points, by the Abel–Jacobi-pinned points dictionary, and the target is separated over a smooth source. It is used in the analysis of the special fibre of $J_1(Mp)$ at $p$, where the compatibility of the Galois action with the component projections and with the diamond operators is established.
